---
import SiteLayout from '../../layouts/SiteLayout.astro';
---

<SiteLayout
  title="The founding vision — Caelora Journal"
  description="The founding note for Caelora: an independent fantasy voxel sandbox, a world-first vision, and an honest account of what comes next."
>
  <main id="main" class="container document">
    <span class="eyebrow">Development journal / Founding note / 15 October 2026</span>
    <h1>Making room for your kind of adventure.</h1>
    <p>
      Caelora starts with a world, rather than a destiny assigned to its player. The guiding idea is
      a medieval-fantasy voxel sandbox where exploration, gathering, survival and building give
      people room to find their own rhythm.
    </p>
    <h2>A place before a prophecy</h2>
    <p>
      We want homes worth returning to, landscapes worth crossing and creatures worth remembering.
      Villages, guilds and kingdoms belong to that vision. Player-created nations and authoritative
      multiplayer are longer-term ambitions, not promises of features available today.
    </p>
    <h2>What exists here today</h2>
    <p>
      This first public website introduces the project and its art direction. The images are concept
      artwork, not gameplay footage. They show a visual ambition; they are not a guarantee of final
      terrain, interfaces, creatures or locations.
    </p>
    <p>
      The game is a separate native Unity 6 project for Windows. The website is its future
      commercial and community platform. No release date or price has been announced, and purchases,
      account registration and launcher downloads are not available here.
    </p>
    <h2>Building the foundations</h2>
    <p>
      The first platform milestone establishes a public site and the technical boundaries for future
      accounts and services. Steam and direct purchases are intended to converge on a single
      ownership model, but neither purchase route is integrated at this stage.
    </p>
    <p>
      Future journal entries should document work that can be demonstrated. This note records the
      direction — and leaves room for development to change it.
    </p>
    <a class="text-link" href="/#world">
      ← Return to the world
    </a>
  </main>
</SiteLayout>
